{
  "case_id": "2006-05-6551",
  "document_hash": "b5adf815700bbb9289ef247844e0de1151ab0aa657e89fbba7b358b565d3289b",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2006-05-6551",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b5adf815700bbb9289ef247844e0de1151ab0aa657e89fbba7b358b565d3289b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b5adf815700b-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:01 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2006-05-6551",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b5adf815700bbb9289ef247844e0de1151ab0aa657e89fbba7b358b565d3289b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b5adf815700b-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: Mr. Gold, I know you take that position on the facts of this case, but is it not true that there could be cases in which the verdict of the jury would establish certain facts that would justify going beyond the middle term? For example, the Black case itself, as the justice who dissented in this case thought the sentence was permissible in that case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2006-05-6551",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b5adf815700bbb9289ef247844e0de1151ab0aa657e89fbba7b358b565d3289b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b5adf815700b-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "An aggravating factor means\naggravating fact."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2006-05-6551",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b5adf815700bbb9289ef247844e0de1151ab0aa657e89fbba7b358b565d3289b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b5adf815700b-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What would you think would be necessary, what would be the least change California would have to make to bring its system into compliance with our decisions?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Gold",
      "case_id": "2006-05-6551",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b5adf815700bbb9289ef247844e0de1151ab0aa657e89fbba7b358b565d3289b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b5adf815700b-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the court could -- the court or the legislature could change section 1170(b) to read something like: \"A judge may impose\" instead of \"shall impose\" the middle term. And that would be valid to the extent that what they mean by \"may\" is they can now impose the middle term based just on the facts found by the jury."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Laurence",
      "case_id": "2006-05-6551",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b5adf815700bbb9289ef247844e0de1151ab0aa657e89fbba7b358b565d3289b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b5adf815700b-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The central lesson from Booker, from the real portion of Booker, is that not every constraint that's placed on a trial court's discretion in selecting a term within a range that requires fact-finding invokes the Sixth Amendment requirement of a jury trial."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2006-05-6551",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b5adf815700bbb9289ef247844e0de1151ab0aa657e89fbba7b358b565d3289b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b5adf815700b-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Is that not correct?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2006-05-6551",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b5adf815700bbb9289ef247844e0de1151ab0aa657e89fbba7b358b565d3289b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b5adf815700b-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Is Black repealing the rule of court?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Laurence",
      "case_id": "2006-05-6551",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b5adf815700bbb9289ef247844e0de1151ab0aa657e89fbba7b358b565d3289b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b5adf815700b-0002-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "California's system as structured, which only requires a reasonableness constraint, does not violate the Constitution."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2006-05-6551",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b5adf815700bbb9289ef247844e0de1151ab0aa657e89fbba7b358b565d3289b",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b5adf815700b-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "May I ask you this question? Excluding capital cases, in your view -- anecdotally, if it has to be that -- what percentage of cases that go to juries, that go to jury trial, result in bifurcated proceedings for sentencing purposes? 10 percent?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2006-05-6551",
      "confidence": 0.0,
